{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>I know.</b> These verses, up to \"and further I saw\" (v. 16), are connected. He already mentioned that there is nothing good for a man except to eat and drink, and he is not speaking about every man, but about the man mentioned who toils day and night to gather wealth. He said that he has from it only what he eats, for it is possible that he is guarding it for one who is good in the eyes of Elohim to take it. And he also mentioned this matter a second time on account of the times, for the matter here concerns the times for everything — to rise to wealth or to fall from it. Nothing can be added to it, and nothing can be taken away. Therefore there is nothing good for the toiler but to rejoice in his portion, or in his wealth to do good in his life, that he may live a good life. Or its meaning may be: to do good in his life, that he may receive a good reward at his death."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "The Hebrew דבקי' is an abbreviation for דבקים, 'connected'; resolved by context."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:27:22.132548263Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}